{
	title: 'bash declares the files it changes; the host snapshots them',
	spec: `The bash tool takes an optional modifies argument: a list of file paths or globs relative to cwd that the command will create, change or delete. Absolute paths, parent traversal, NULs and non-string entries are refused before execution. The system prompt tells the model to declare it for every command that writes files. Before running, the host expands globs (plus literal paths that do not exist yet), snapshots each file into content-addressed raw-byte blobs capped at 1 MB; larger or sensitive files retain size and mtime only. After exit (including failure, timeout or cancellation), it expands the globs again to include newly created matches, snapshots again and appends one file_changes history record for a declared call, including only files whose snapshots changed; an unchanged call has an empty files list. Record shape: {toolId, cwd, files: [{path, before, after}]}, with before/after SHA-256 hashes, null for absence, or {size, mtime} for metadata-only files. Sensitive paths are checked by shared approval.sensitive for both lexical and real paths: .env and .env.*, .ssh/.aws/.gnupg, credentials and credentials.*, the home credential store, id_rsa/id_dsa/id_ecdsa/id_ed25519 and .pub variants, and .pem/.key/.p12/.pfx extensions. Sensitive and non-regular files retain no bytes. Content-addressed blobs are owner-only under the session file-blobs directory and survive subsequent edits/deletions. File-change records do not enter provider input and are hidden until a diff-view task renders them.
Undeclared changes are observed cheaply too: in a Git work tree the host asynchronously runs git status --porcelain=v1 -z --untracked-files=all before and after every bash call, including calls without modifies. Paths whose status changed and were not declared are included as {path, undeclared: true, statusBefore, statusAfter}, with no before/after content; null status means no porcelain entry. Rename source and destination are both observed. Same-status dirty-file edits and ignored files cannot be detected by this inexpensive mechanism. Hal calls these observed changes during the call, not proof of authorship.
Host-wide, calls whose expanded modifies sets overlap by realpath run one at a time across sessions; missing literals are canonicalized through their nearest existing ancestor. Identical empty globs and glob/literal overlaps reserve expressions too. The second call waits before its before-snapshot, re-expands after the first releases, and its tool block shows waiting for <session-id> (editing <path>). A wait honors cancellation without running the command; disjoint calls and calls without modifies never wait for another call. A background call keeps its snapshot and locks until actual process exit, not the early started-in-background result; this holds for its own session too. Waiting is visible for foreground and background calls; a wait on a running background job names the job (#<block>) and that it must exit, so a session waiting on its own job is explicable.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'API for fz/0f: src/common/replay.ts exports FileSnapshot = SHA-256 string | {size:number,mtime:number milliseconds} | null, and FileChange = declared {path,before,after} or undeclared {path,undeclared:true,statusBefore:string|null,statusAfter:string|null} without before/after. HistoryRecord adds type:file_changes with toolId (tool call id), cwd, files, n and ts. Paths are relative to cwd; Git observations may be outside cwd if another part of the same work tree changed. Before/after null means absent only on declared entries. Records are ignored by replay.toMessages and transcript.recordShown, ready for fz to render.',
		'src/host/file-changes.ts exports mutable fileChanges: validate(input)->normalized patterns; begin(ToolContext,patterns)->Promise<Observation> acquiring host-wide locks then before snapshots/status; finish(Observation)->Promise<void> appends record and releases in finally. Observation holds ctx/patterns/before Map/path snapshots/status Map/release. acquire(ctx,patterns) returns release; state.locks is host-wide. blobPath(sessionId,hash) validates the 64 lowercase hex hash and session id and returns sessions/<id>/file-blobs/<hash>; bytes are raw (including empty/binary files), SHA-256-addressed and owner-only. Consumers can read this path asynchronously; metadata-only entries have no blob. maxBytes() defaults to 1000000. This snapshot namespace is separate from upload/output blobs, whose ids have a different fixed format.',
		'6f had no sensitive-path list in this build, so 8w supplies the shared overridable approval.sensitive(path) policy. It is best effort, not a secret classifier or sandbox. Untracked files use --untracked-files=all rather than directory summaries; porcelain -z renames consume destination then source. Canonicalize cwd for Git-relative paths and waiting labels: macOS /var and /private/var aliases otherwise produce duplicate undeclared entries. Git status cannot identify an edit whose dirty status stays the same; that limitation is intentional and pinned by a behavior test.',
		'Validation: five integration tests cover snapshots/deletion/new glob matches/undeclared observations, metadata-only sensitive and large files, realpath overlap and cancellable waits with disjoint/no-declaration concurrency, background lock lifetime, and invalid input/failing commands. Full ./test after rebase passes all except startup timing under this multi-agent run; the unchanged origin/main baseline fails that same startup test even when run alone (joining UI 188 ms against 150 ms). TypeScript and lint pass. This is a measured baseline timing failure, not a waived behavioral regression.',
		"Invariant restored: a wait message must name what actually holds the lock. A session that started a long-lived background job (edit plus server in one command) later waited on itself, shown as 'Waiting for tab 1 to finish editing', which read as a self-deadlock. The wait now names the background job and the exit it waits for."
	],
	needs: ['1r', 'bz']
}
